  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 1245 AM Monday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Thursday through Friday remains the best chance of \nprecipitation, although guidance continues to show limited rainfall.\n\nOn the synoptic scale, not much has changed regarding the late week \nsystem as the polar jet stream digs south and downstream \ncyclogenesis occurs Thursday into Friday. A southern stream wave \nwill skirt to our south Wednesday, but only bring mid and high \nclouds. The suite of ensemble guidance, including machine-learning \nmodels/ensembles, continue to favor a northward trend in the surface \nlow. The consensus shows a track across northern VA into New \nEngland. This forecast track would offer limited rain chances as the \nmain energy focuses well to the north of central NC. The best chance \nwould ultimately be with the warm front early Thursday and cold \nfront Thursday afternoon and evening. Even with the cold front, \nensembles do not show much rainfall, with the 25th to 75th \npercentile only ranging from a few hundredths to at most three \ntenths of an inch. The main impact from the system will be the \ncolder air and gusty winds. In fact, northwest wind gusts of 30-35 \nmph are favored Fri/Sat with a tight pressure gradient.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Patchy frost may be possible Tuesday morning, but \nwidespread frost and freeze is expected this coming weekend into \nearly next week.\n\nToday, a weak dry cold front slides south later today and tonight, \nwith cool high pressure settling in for early Tuesday. While the \nairmass does cool, it does not appear favorable for widespread \nfrost. Certainly outlying areas will dip into the middle 30s, but \nmost urban areas may only drop into the upper 30s to low 40s. Given \nthat, our confidence was too low to hoist a Frost Advisory. But \nsubsequent shifts may want to reevaluate things with newer guidance. \n\nThe better chance of frost/freeze conditions will come this weekend \ninto early next week. In the wake of the departing system, \nContinental Polar high pressure is forecast to settle in from the TN \nvalley. Corresponding low-level thicknesses drop to between 1300 and \n1310m, supportive of low to middle 30s, with outlying areas in the \nupper 20s. The one limiting factor will be wind gusts. With a tight \npressure gradient expected Fri night between the departing low and \nhigh pressure ridge to our west, lows in the mid to upper 30s may be \nthe norm with limited freezing temperatures. The better threat \nappears Sunday and Monday mornings, when the surface high builds \neast and winds relax.",
